{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Correction to my own correction #1, caught by the plan-review gate. Worth recording because it is the same class of error I flagged in the draft.\n\nI wrote that \"every landed ADR is Accepted\" and that `grep -l \"^\\*\\*Status:\\*\\* Proposed\" docs/adr/0*.md` \"returns nothing on origin/main\". The grep does return nothing — but that is an artefact of the `^\\*\\*` anchor, not evidence that no Proposed ADR exists. **ADR 0071** (`0071-editing-prompt-contracts-from-the-editor.md:5`) is Proposed:\n\n```\n- **Status:** Proposed (design spike — recommendation; implementation deferred to the\n  follow-on stories in §Consequences)\n```\n\nIt is a bulleted list item, so the anchored pattern excludes it. I asserted a universal from a grep whose anchor silently narrowed the search — which is exactly the failure mode I flagged in draft item 4, where a citation was trusted without opening the file.\n\n**The decision does not change, but the reasoning does.** 0117 still lands as `Accepted`, on a narrower and better-supported basis: the sole Proposed precedent is an explicit *design spike* with implementation deferred, whereas 0117 records a decision the operator has already adopted, against which eleven children are filed, and whose own body says the consequences \"are not aspirations\". \"Proposed\" would contradict the document. That is a real argument; \"there are no Proposed ADRs\" was not, because it was false.\n\nAC2 has been rewritten accordingly. It previously asserted the non-existent convention and carried a self-contradictory proof — it required the anchored Proposed count to be \"unchanged from origin/main\" while the ticket described authoring 0117 as Proposed, so the proof would have failed precisely if the plan had been followed as written. It now asserts the ADR's own line directly (`grep -q '^\\*\\*Status:\\*\\* Accepted' docs/adr/0117-…md`) and separately pins 0071 as untouched.\n\nAlso actioned from the same review, on S10 rather than here: the proving grep `grep -n \"not yet a declared support target\" .github/workflows/test.yml` is **vacuous** — the sentence wraps across lines 331/332, so the joined phrase matches nothing even on the uncorrected tree (`git show origin/main:… | grep -c` returns `0`). My regression test had inherited that flaw and has been hardened to join and whitespace-normalise the comment lines before matching; the fix is proven by restoring `origin/main`'s wrapped block verbatim and watching the test fail, which it did not do before."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788576306594899507,"uuid":"4e3f7d51-c424-47c5-ba22-846f329b42f4"}